{
  "configuration_id": "config_048",
  "reason": "Evidence: The largest performance jump in the study came from switching the RNA encoder from PCA n2000 to SCVI n200 in the multimodal/multiprofile/avgpool/concat setting (+8.6 pp: 0.629 → 0.715), establishing that the learned RNA encoder is the dominant design lever. Uncertainty: We have only tested one learned encoder (SCVI n200); it is unknown whether SCGPT—a different learned encoder with a different inductive bias—would match or exceed SCVI, or whether the gain is specific to SCVI's architecture. What this tests: config_048 changes only the RNA encoder from SCVI n200 to SCGPT while holding all other settings at their empirically best values (ViT-L, multiprofile, avgpool, concat), giving a clean single-variable comparison. Why it is the best next experiment: The encoder choice showed the largest effect size observed, so resolving whether SCGPT ≥ SCVI or SCVI > SCGPT is the highest-value uncertainty to reduce. A positive result opens a new promising direction; a negative result confirms SCVI as the encoder of choice and redirects effort to aggregation/fusion refinements. This is more informative than testing a small aggregation or fusion swap on the already-strong SCVI config, where expected gains are likely ≤3 pp based on the PCA-era evidence."
}

